Why AI sometimes “ignores” the rules

It's usually not literally deciding:

“I understand the rule and I'm going to disobey it.”

That's an important distinction.

A language model is generating the next action/token based on a huge learned probability distribution conditioned on its context.

It doesn't inherently have:

a persistent executive function
a guaranteed perfect memory
a perfect internal project model
a formal understanding of every instruction
a deterministic requirements engine
a reliable notion of “done”
an objective visual-quality function
guaranteed access to every source of truth
guaranteed self-criticism
guaranteed verification
guaranteed persistence of lessons between tasks

So you can tell it:

“This is the law.”

And it can still produce something that violates the law.

Not because it is malicious.

Because instructions are inputs to a generative system, not necessarily constraints on an execution engine.

That's the distinction.

And here's the really important part

You cannot solve this simply by making the instruction document longer.

In fact, eventually the opposite happens.

You can have:

10 laws → useful

50 laws → stronger

200 laws → potentially overwhelming

1,000 laws → potentially counterproductive

because now you've created an enormous textual specification that the model must continuously keep in working context while simultaneously reasoning, coding, navigating files, interpreting requirements, making aesthetic decisions, etc.

More instructions ≠ more reliability.

What you actually want is architecture.

